  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><strong data-start=\"80\" data-end=\"117\">TSH (Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone)</strong> is produced by the <strong data-start=\"137\" data-end=\"156\">pituitary gland</strong> and regulates the production of <strong data-start=\"189\" data-end=\"221\">thyroid hormones (T3 and T4)</strong> by the thyroid gland. The <strong data-start=\"248\" data-end=\"290\">ultrasensitive TSH test (uTSH or sTSH)</strong> is highly sensitive and can detect even minor changes in thyroid function.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p data-start=\"1136\" data-end=\"1162\"><strong data-start=\"1140\" data-end=\"1162\">Clinical Scenarios</strong></p>\n<p data-start=\"1136\" data-end=\"1162\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p data-start=\"1164\" data-end=\"1196\"><strong data-start=\"1169\" data-end=\"1196\">1. Low TSH (&lt;0.4 mIU/L)</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"1197\" data-end=\"1491\">\n<li data-start=\"1197\" data-end=\"1413\">\n<p data-start=\"1199\" data-end=\"1219\"><strong data-start=\"1199\" data-end=\"1218\">Possible causes</strong>:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"1222\" data-end=\"1413\">\n<li data-start=\"1222\" data-end=\"1297\">\n<p data-start=\"1224\" data-end=\"1297\">Primary <strong data-start=\"1232\" data-end=\"1251\">hyperthyroidism</strong> (e.g., Graves&rsquo; disease, toxic nodular goiter)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1300\" data-end=\"1343\">\n<p data-start=\"1302\" data-end=\"1343\">Excessive <strong data-start=\"1312\" data-end=\"1343\">thyroid hormone replacement</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1346\" data-end=\"1367\">\n<p data-start=\"1348\" data-end=\"1367\"><strong data-start=\"1348\" data-end=\"1367\">Early pregnancy</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1370\" data-end=\"1413\">\n<p data-start=\"1372\" data-end=\"1413\">Pituitary/hypothalamic dysfunction (rare)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1415\" data-end=\"1491\">\n<p data-start=\"1417\" data-end=\"1491\"><strong data-start=\"1417\" data-end=\"1436\">Further testing</strong>: Free T4 and Free T3 levels to confirm hyperthyroidism</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. High TSH (&gt;10 mIU/L)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"1954\" data-end=\"1990\">\n<p data-start=\"1956\" data-end=\"1990\">Indicates <strong data-start=\"1966\" data-end=\"1990\">overt hypothyroidism</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1991\" data-end=\"2094\">\n<p data-start=\"1993\" data-end=\"2094\">Common causes: <strong data-start=\"2008\" data-end=\"2035\">Hashimoto&rsquo;s thyroiditis</strong>, post-thyroidectomy, or inadequate thyroid hormone therapy</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"2095\" data-end=\"2139\">\n<p data-start=\"2097\" data-end=\"2139\">Usually warrants <strong data-start=\"2114\" data-end=\"2139\">levothyroxine therapy</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>TSH Value</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Possible Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&lt;0.4 mIU/L</strong></td>\n<td>Suggests <strong>hyperthyroidism</strong> or <strong>suppressed TSH</strong> (low TSH)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>0.4 &ndash; 4.0 mIU/L</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Normal thyroid function</strong> (euthyroid)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4.1 &ndash; 10.0 mIU/L</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mild/subclinical hypothyroidism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&gt;10.0 mIU/L</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Overt hypothyroidism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
